questions.criteria:
1 point per valid reason, up to 2:
- a car park has a shallow setting / does not extend deep underground;
- a car park is not multifunctional (earthscrapers are multifunctional designs);
- no actual earthscrapers have been built yet — they exist only in fiction and architectural plans, whereas car parks are real.

1 point per valid benefit, up to 2:
- it would help solve the problems of restricted space and a growing population (extra living, office and retail space);
- it would conserve the city's threatened historic buildings;
- it would provide cultural facilities (a 10-floor museum).

1 point per valid point, up to 3:
- building deep underground brings potentially significant engineering problems;
- Mexico's hot climate means the immense glass top could act as a greenhouse, so adequate ventilation throughout would be needed;
- it would demand the very best in human ingenuity / highly skilled teams to oversee and implement the project.

1 point per valid point expressed in own words, up to 3:
- the claim that they would be environmentally friendly / need little energy for heating and cooling is not widely believed;
- governments have other spending priorities to weigh up;
- they would inevitably increase traffic congestion and pollution above ground.
No credit for lifting whole phrases from the text.
